{"subject":"Update: White House Counsel Maintains Objection to HSGAC Subpoena Package & Standing Instructions","body":"Following up on the revision removing the "Executive Privilege" label: White House Counsel's Office confirmed that while removing the Executive Privilege designation resolves that specific point (and it takes no position on a deliberative-process-only assertion over Category C), its objection to clearing the package and standing instructions remains in place.

Specifically, White House Counsel reiterated that it will not clear the package or concur in the standing instructions unless a revised package is prepared with Division of General Law counsel that:
1. Accurately describes Category A (DOI-HSGAC-000001 to 000187), including AFS participation and subject matter, or produces it;
2. Produces or properly logs the six responsive emails (DOI-HSGAC-000272 to 000295); and
3. Conforms the transmittal letter to what is actually produced and logged.

Because the Secretary's Office indicated the Secretary will not sign until a cleared package is presented, and the DGL Records-Review/Production Teams require written direction from the Deputy Solicitor or Solicitor addressing both DGL counsel's recommendation and White House Counsel's position before adopting the standing instructions or proceeding, the package remains on hold pending further decision by the Deputy Solicitor and your office.","to":["Office of the Secretary - Chief of Staff"]}

